---
import { PilaresLab } from "../../components/PilaresLab.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/PilaresLab.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
import "../../components/PainelDaCarga.css";
import "@ovh/depth-ui/src/stage.css";
import "../../components/Explorer.css";
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="Three pillars, one blind spot — OpenLabs"
  description="Three recorders watch the same requests and keep different things. Ask them which requests were slow, and only one can answer — because a signal is a decision about what to throw away, and what is thrown away at write time cannot be asked for later."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>Three recorders, one event, three different memories</h1>
        <p>
          Every introduction presents traces, metrics and logs as three kinds of
          data. They are not. They are three decisions about <b>what to throw
          away</b> — taken on the same event, at the moment it is written — and
          what one of them discards, no backend can hand back to you later.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below, one service handles requests. Three recorders watch the exact
          same ones — and <b>clicking any item on a wire follows it</b>: the
          panel shows what each recorder is about to keep of that one request,
          and the fields that vanish on the way in are the ones you will not be
          able to ask for later. Let it run, then ask the question at the bottom: <em>which
          requests took longer than 250&nbsp;ms?</em> One of the three answers
          with a list. One answers with a number, at best. One answers only if
          the person who wrote the code happened to mention it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>One tick is one second.</p>
        <p>Nothing here is scripted: every row is the run.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <PilaresLab client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <p>
          Three moves, and each one is a thing that happens to real systems.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Turn off &ldquo;the code logs every request&rdquo;.</b> The log
            keeps only failures, and the counter of requests it said nothing
            about starts climbing. That number is the part of your traffic no
            log will ever describe — and the decision was taken in the editor,
            not in configuration.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Add <code>user.id</code> to the metric attributes.</b> Watch the
            series count climb: one attribute is not one more column, it is one
            more series <em>per distinct value</em>. Then drop the cardinality
            limit to 6 and watch the overflow row appear — nothing is lost and
            nothing is double-counted, and the breakdown you wanted is gone.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Ask for 300&nbsp;ms instead of 250.</b> The metric stops being
            able to answer even <em>how many</em>: 300 falls inside a bucket, and
            a bucket is a count, not a list. The buckets were chosen before the
            first measurement — that is what makes the loss irreversible.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>
          Answer first, then run it. Predicting and being wrong is what makes the
          answer stick.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="A histogram says 12 requests landed above 500 ms. You need to know which ones. Where do you get them?"
          opcoes={[
            "Nowhere — the metric never knew, and no backend can recover it",
            "From the metric backend: it stores the raw values behind the buckets",
            "From the metric, if you enable high-resolution storage",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="A histogram bucket holds a count. The values that produced it were discarded when the measurement was recorded — in the process, before anything was exported. That is not compression, so there is nothing to decompress: the only mechanical path from an aggregate back to an individual request is an exemplar, and it is a sample kept on purpose, not a recovery."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Read the theory</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel/articles/what-a-signal-is")}
            >What a signal is</a
          > argues the same thing in prose, from first principles — and
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel/articles/the-seam-between-signals")}
            >The seam between the signals</a
          > picks up where this lab leaves off: once you know each signal has its
          own blind spot, the work is the crossing between them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
